\subsection{Refs that land on a trailer commit}

Tags and other refs are created by whatever is at HEAD when the
user asks, and HEAD is a trailer commit for as long as no new run
has started.
A tag created just after a run finished therefore points at a
commit that no listing contains.
Since a trailer commit exists only to complete the run before it,
we treat such a ref as naming that run, and step back to its
header commit.

The hash reaching this function is whatever a ref or a caller
supplied, so the subject probe can fail, and its failing is not an
error here.
Git refuses a hash the object database no longer has, and a ref may
name an object it will not walk as a commit at all; the probe then
exits non-zero and [[git()]] would raise [[GitError]]
(\cref{sec:diagnostics}).
That failure is itself the answer to the question being asked: a hash
Git cannot read as a commit is certainly not a trailer commit.
So the probe opts out of [[check]] and such a hash resolves to
itself---the same answer the [[rev-parse]] probe below already gives
when it cannot step back to a parent.

Reporting \enquote{not a commit} to the caller instead, as [[None]] or
as an exception, has no shape that suits the callers.
Both of them are read paths that have to degrade rather than abort:
[[get_tag_map]] (\cref{sec:tag-resolution}), whose own [[git tag -l]]
already falls back to an empty map, and [[play --from]]
(\cref{sec:play}), which turns a hash it cannot find in the commit
list into a message naming the ref the user typed.
Handing them back the hash unchanged leads each of them to the answer
it already has for a hash that names no run, whereas a second return
shape would give both of them a new failure mode to handle for
nothing gained.

<<commits functions>>=
def resolve_record_commit(repo, commit_hash):
    """Return the commit that carries ``commit_hash``'s record.

    Trailer commits resolve to the header commit they complete;
    every other hash resolves to itself, including one that Git
    will not read as a commit.
    """
    probe = repo.git(
        "log", "-1", "--format=%s", commit_hash, check=False,
    )
    if probe.returncode != 0:
        return commit_hash
    if not probe.stdout.strip().startswith(TRAILER_PREFIX):
        return commit_hash

    parent = repo.git(
        "rev-parse", f"{commit_hash}^", check=False,
    )
    if parent.returncode != 0:
        return commit_hash
    return parent.stdout.strip()
@

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_resolve_record_commit_maps_trailer_to_header(workdir):
    from learnlog.commits import resolve_record_commit
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    repo.begin_run(
        "hello.py", [], datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0),
    )
    header = repo.git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 1),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    trailer = repo.git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
    assert resolve_record_commit(repo, trailer) == header
    assert resolve_record_commit(repo, header) == header
@

The all-zero hash stands in for every object the probe cannot read:
it is well formed, so it reaches Git rather than being rejected on
the way, and no repository has it.
Answering with the hash rather than raising is what the callers above
depend on.

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_resolve_record_commit_tolerates_unreadable_hash(workdir):
    """A hash Git cannot read as a commit resolves to itself."""
    from learnlog.commits import resolve_record_commit
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    absent = "0" * 40
    assert resolve_record_commit(repo, absent) == absent
@


\section{Elapsed time between runs}

Teachers often care not only about \emph{what} a student ran, but also
about how long they paused before the next attempt.
The playback viewer only needs a lightweight approximation, so we use the
commit author date that [[begin_run]] records at run start.

That approximation is good enough for a quick \enquote{instant retry vs.
deliberate pause} signal, but it is still slightly noisy.
Interrupted runs may never record an end time, and playback works from Git
commit summaries rather than full run blocks.
The helper here therefore measures the interval from the previous displayed
student commit to the current one, while the ProgSnap2 analyser computes the
more precise end-to-start interval from the run data itself.

\subsection{Parsing ISO timestamps defensively}

The run logs and Git commit dates both use ISO timestamps.
We treat empty or malformed strings as missing data rather than raising, so
the viewer can simply omit the elapsed-time line when the underlying data is
incomplete.

<<commits functions>>=
def _parse_iso(value):
    """Return ``datetime.fromisoformat(value)`` or ``None``.

    Empty or invalid timestamps are treated as missing.
    """
    if not value:
        return None
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(value)
    except ValueError:
        return None
@

Let's verify that valid timestamps parse and invalid ones quietly disappear:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_parse_iso_valid_and_invalid():
    from learnlog.commits import _parse_iso
    assert _parse_iso("2026-01-01T10:00:00") == \
        datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
    assert _parse_iso("") is None
    assert _parse_iso("not-a-date") is None
@


\subsection{Formatting elapsed time for humans}

Playback and analysis both need the same human-readable rendering.
We keep the format compact so it fits naturally in a one-line status field:
seconds for short retries, minutes and seconds for ordinary pauses, and hours
and minutes for longer breaks.

Negative intervals can happen if clocks skew or imported data is inconsistent.
Rather than showing a confusing negative duration, we clamp anything below one
second to [[< 1 s]].

<<commits functions>>=
def format_elapsed(seconds):
    """Return a compact human-readable elapsed time.

    Returns ``""`` for ``None``.  Values below one second,
    including negative ones, are rendered as ``"< 1 s"``.
    """
    if seconds is None:
        return ""

    if seconds < 1:
        return "< 1 s"

    total = int(seconds)
    if total < 60:
        return f"{total} s"

    if total < 3600:
        minutes, secs = divmod(total, 60)
        if secs == 0:
            return f"{minutes} min"
        return f"{minutes} min {secs} s"

    hours = total // 3600
    minutes = (total % 3600) // 60
    return f"{hours} h {minutes} min"
@

We can pin down the display choices with a few representative cases:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_format_elapsed_human_readable():
    from learnlog.commits import format_elapsed
    assert format_elapsed(None) == ""
    assert format_elapsed(-5) == "< 1 s"
    assert format_elapsed(0.2) == "< 1 s"
    assert format_elapsed(5) == "5 s"
    assert format_elapsed(120) == "2 min"
    assert format_elapsed(135) == "2 min 15 s"
    assert format_elapsed(3780) == "1 h 3 min"
@


\subsection{Recognising learnlog's own commits}

A [[Run: learnlog ...]] block can mean two quite different things to
whoever reads the log.
[[learnlog play]] and [[learnlog list]] are the student stepping back
through their own history, and the recorder logs exactly those two
subcommands because that reflection is itself evidence of learning
(\cref{learnlog}); every view therefore shows them by default.
Everything else learnlog leaves behind is plumbing: the [[pip]] install
that [[learnlog init]] performs, and---in repositories written before
the recorder narrowed to review runs---records of the remaining
subcommands, which used to log themselves as well.
That kind is noise in every view, so it is what [[--all]] now reveals.

Three predicates therefore share one text-matching core, and each view
picks the one that matches its question.
[[is_learnlog_command]] asks \enquote{is this learnlog at all}, which
is what the elapsed indicator needs; [[is_review_command]] picks out
the reflection runs; [[is_internal_command]] is the remainder, and is
the one the display filters use.

All three read the command line rather than the commit, so the same
test serves a commit subject (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}) and a parsed
run block (\cref{progsnap2}).

Installing learnlog counts as learnlog activity too.
[[learnlog init]] pip-installs the package into the project venv, and
that [[pip]] run is captured like any other; it is setup, not
development, so it is hidden by the same flag.
We match only [[pip]] commands that mention learnlog, so a student
installing their own dependencies still sees those runs.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_learnlog_command(command):
    """Return true if ``command`` is learnlog's own activity.

    Covers ``learnlog`` itself and the ``pip`` invocation that
    installs it during ``learnlog init``.
    """
    parts = command.split()
    if not parts:
        return False
    if parts[0] == "learnlog":
        return True
    return parts[0] in INSTALLER_COMMANDS and any(
        "learnlog" in argument for argument in parts[1:]
    )
<<commits constants>>=
INSTALLER_COMMANDS = ("pip", "pip3")
@

A commit records learnlog activity when either half of a run record
does: the header names the command, and the trailer repeats it, so
whichever commit we are looking at answers the question.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_learnlog_commit(commit):
    """Return true if ``commit`` records a learnlog CLI run."""
    subject = commit.get("subject", "")
    for prefix in (RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX, TRAILER_PREFIX):
        if subject.startswith(prefix):
            return is_learnlog_command(
                subject[len(prefix):]
            )
    return False
<<commits constants>>=
RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX = "Run: "
@

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_is_learnlog_commit():
    from learnlog.commits import is_learnlog_commit
    assert is_learnlog_commit({"subject": "Run: learnlog play"})
    assert is_learnlog_commit(
        {"subject": "Run-End: learnlog play"}
    )
    assert is_learnlog_commit(
        {"subject": "Run: pip install learnlog"}
    )
    assert not is_learnlog_commit({"subject": "Run: hello.py"})
    assert not is_learnlog_commit(
        {"subject": "Run: pip install requests"}
    )
    assert not is_learnlog_commit(
        {"subject": "learnlog: initial commit"}
    )
@

A review run is recognised by its subcommand, which makes the list of
review subcommands a piece of vocabulary that the recorder and this
reader must agree on: the recorder consults it to decide whether to
log an invocation at all (\cref{learnlog}), and disagreement would
mean runs that are recorded and then hidden, or filtered and never
written.
So [[REVIEW_SUBCOMMANDS]] is not defined here: it comes from
\cref{gitrepo} through the import above, exactly like
[[RUN_ID_FIELD]] and [[TRAILER_PREFIX]]---one definition that both
halves read is what makes disagreement impossible rather than merely
unlikely (\cref{sec:review-subcommands}).

Only the first two tokens decide.
Anything after them is options and arguments ([[learnlog list --all]]
is still a review run), and a bare [[learnlog]] with no subcommand is
not one---the recorder does not log it either.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_review_command(command):
    """Return true if ``command`` is a learnlog review run.

    Review runs are ``learnlog play`` and ``learnlog list``:
    the subcommands with which a student looks back at their
    own history.
    """
    parts = command.split()
    return (
        len(parts) > 1
        and parts[0] == "learnlog"
        and parts[1] in REVIEW_SUBCOMMANDS
    )
@

The internal test is the difference between the two: learnlog's own
activity minus the part of it that belongs to the student.
Writing it as a difference rather than as a second list of subcommands
means a subcommand can never be both, and that a repository recorded by
an older version---which logged subcommands this list has never heard
of---still classifies every one of them as plumbing.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_internal_command(command):
    """Return true if ``command`` is learnlog's own plumbing.

    Learnlog activity that is not a review run: the ``pip``
    install performed by ``learnlog init``, and records of
    other subcommands left by versions that logged them.
    """
    return (
        is_learnlog_command(command)
        and not is_review_command(command)
    )
@

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_is_review_command():
    from learnlog.commits import (
        is_internal_command,
        is_review_command,
    )
    assert is_review_command("learnlog play")
    assert is_review_command("learnlog list --all")
    assert not is_review_command("learnlog")
    assert not is_review_command("learnlog export out.bundle")
    assert not is_review_command("pip install learnlog")
    assert not is_review_command("play.py list")
    assert not is_internal_command("learnlog play")
    assert is_internal_command("learnlog export out.bundle")
    assert is_internal_command("pip install learnlog")
    assert not is_internal_command("hello.py")
@


\subsection{Computing playback gaps}

The playback view walks commit summaries, not parsed run blocks, so the best
shared signal is the difference between commit author dates.
We look backwards for the previous commit that actually \emph{records a
student run}---its subject begins [[Run:]] but is not one of learnlog's own
[[Run: learnlog ...]] invocations---and subtract its date from the current
commit's date.

Review runs are skipped here deliberately, even though every other view
now shows them: this number is meant to be read as thinking time between
attempts at the code, and time spent paging through the log is not an
attempt.
The reference point is therefore chosen with [[is_learnlog_commit]], the
widest of the three predicates, rather than with the narrower internal
test that the displays filter by.

Since every run now has a header commit of its own
(\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}), that date is the run's own start
time.  It used to be the date of whichever run first created the commit
that later runs were amended into, which made every gap after the first
one wrong.

Requiring a [[Run:]] subject is what excludes the [[learnlog: initial
commit]].
That commit's author date is the project-setup time, and no program had run
yet, so treating it as the previous run would label setup time as think time
on the very first run.
Skipping it means the first run simply shows no gap, which is correct: there
was nothing before it.
If either timestamp is missing or malformed, we return [[None]] so the caller
simply omits the line.

<<commits functions>>=
def elapsed_since_previous_run(commits, index):
    """Return seconds since the previous student run.

    The interval is measured from the most recent earlier
    commit that records a student run (its subject starts
    with ``Run:`` and is not a ``Run: learnlog`` invocation)
    to ``commits[index]``'s date.  The initial commit is
    skipped, so the first run reports ``None``.  Returns
    ``None`` when no such previous commit exists or either
    timestamp cannot be parsed.
    """
    if index <= 0 or index >= len(commits):
        return None

    current = _parse_iso(commits[index].get("date"))
    if current is None:
        return None

    for previous_index in range(index - 1, -1, -1):
        previous_commit = commits[previous_index]
        subject = previous_commit.get("subject", "")
        if not subject.startswith(RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX) or \
                is_learnlog_commit(previous_commit):
            continue
        previous = _parse_iso(previous_commit.get("date"))
        if previous is None:
            return None
        return (current - previous).total_seconds()

    return None
@

The helper skips internal learnlog commits when looking for the reference
point, so a teacher sees the gap between student attempts rather than the gap
between a run and a later [[learnlog play]] command:

<<commits test functions>>=
