\section{Commit details}

For a single commit we use [[git show]], which produces the
commit header, the full message (containing the captured I/O),
and the unified diff (showing the code that changed)---all in a
single subprocess call.

A teacher reviewing student work typically wants to see
\emph{what changed} before reading the run metadata (script
name, arguments, captured I/O).
The default [[git show]] order is header, message, diff; we
rearrange to header, diff, message so that code changes appear
first.


\subsection{Parsing [[git show]] sections}

The [[git show]] output always starts with metadata lines
(commit hash, author, date) followed by a blank line, then the
indented commit message, then the diff.
Both reordering and filtering need to locate these three sections,
so we extract the boundary-finding into a single function.

The header ends at the first blank line.
The diff starts at the first [[diff --git]] line; when no diff is
present---for example, the initial commit that only records a run
without any file changes---[[diff_start]] is [[None]].

<<commits functions>>=
def parse_show_sections(lines):
    """Find section boundaries in ``git show`` output.

    Returns ``(header_end, diff_start)`` where
    ``header_end`` is the index of the first blank line and
    ``diff_start`` is the index of the first ``diff --git``
    line, or ``None`` if no diff is present.
    """
    header_end = 0
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line == "":
            header_end = i
            break

    diff_start = None
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line.startswith("diff --git "):
            diff_start = i
            break

    return header_end, diff_start
@


\subsection{Reordering commit detail}

Given the section boundaries, rearranging is a matter of slicing the
lines into header, message, and diff, then reassembling them in the
order that puts code changes first.
When no diff is present (the initial commit), we leave the output
unchanged.

<<commits functions>>=
def reorder_commit_detail(detail):
    """Reorder ``git show`` output: header, diff, message.

    Moves the unified diff before the commit message so that
    code changes are visible first in the viewer.
    """
    lines = detail.split("\n")
    header_end, diff_start = parse_show_sections(lines)

    if diff_start is None:
        return detail

    header = lines[:header_end + 1]
    message = lines[header_end + 1:diff_start]
    diff = lines[diff_start:]

    return "\n".join(header + diff + [""] + message)
@


\section{Filtering learnlog's internal runs}
\label{sec:filter-learnlog}

Two kinds of block in a log are not the student running their own
program, and they deserve opposite treatment.
A review run is one of them, and it stays: that a student went back
through their history the evening before the deadline is part of the
story a reader came for.
The other kind is plumbing---the [[pip]] install that
[[learnlog init]] performs, and whatever an older version recorded of
the subcommands that no longer log themselves.
Plumbing is noise in every view, so it is hidden unless the user asks
for it with [[--all]].

Filtering happens at the run-block level within each commit message
rather than at the commit level, because a message can carry several
blocks (\cref{sec:block-sep}) and a mixed message must keep its student
runs.

<<commits functions>>=
def filter_internal_runs(message):
    """Remove learnlog's internal run blocks from a message.

    Splits on the ``---`` block separator and removes blocks
    whose header records learnlog's own plumbing.  Review runs
    (``learnlog play`` and ``learnlog list``) are kept, as are
    the student's own programs.  Returns the filtered message,
    which may be empty if every block was internal.
    """
    blocks = message.split(BLOCK_SEP)
    filtered = [
        b for b in blocks if not _is_internal_block(b)
    ]
    return BLOCK_SEP.join(filtered)
@

A block's first line is its [[Run:]] header, which carries the same
command string as the commit subject---so the block-level and the
commit-level tests ask [[is_internal_command]] the same question about
the same text.

<<commits functions>>=
def _is_internal_block(block):
    """True if ``block`` records learnlog's own plumbing."""
    first_line = block.lstrip().split("\n", 1)[0]
    if not first_line.startswith(RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX):
        return False
    return is_internal_command(
        first_line[len(RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX):]
    )
@

The exporter (\cref{progsnap2}) asks the same questions about a run it
has already parsed into fields, where the command is split in two.
Rebuilding the command line from those fields is the only part that
differs from the block-level and commit-level tests, so it is the only
part written once and shared by all three run-level predicates:

<<commits functions>>=
def _run_command(run):
    """Rebuild the command line of a parsed run block."""
    script = run.get("script") or ""
    arguments = run.get("arguments") or ""
    return f"{script} {arguments}"
@

<<commits functions>>=
def is_learnlog_run(run):
    """True if a parsed run block records learnlog's activity."""
    return is_learnlog_command(_run_command(run))
@

<<commits functions>>=
def is_review_run(run):
    """True if a parsed run block records a review run."""
    return is_review_command(_run_command(run))
@

<<commits functions>>=
def is_internal_run(run):
    """True if a parsed run block records learnlog plumbing."""
    return is_internal_command(_run_command(run))
@

A parsed block with no command at all---a fragment, or a block the
parser could make nothing of---is nobody's activity, so every one of
the three answers false rather than guessing:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_is_learnlog_run_parsed_block():
    from learnlog.commits import is_learnlog_run
    assert is_learnlog_run(
        {"script": "learnlog", "arguments": "play"}
    )
    assert is_learnlog_run(
        {"script": "pip", "arguments": "install learnlog"}
    )
    assert not is_learnlog_run(
        {"script": "hello.py", "arguments": "(none)"}
    )
    assert not is_learnlog_run(
        {"script": None, "arguments": None}
    )

def test_is_internal_run():
    from learnlog.commits import (
        is_internal_run,
        is_review_run,
    )
    assert is_internal_run(
        {"script": "pip", "arguments": "install learnlog"}
